    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d214-a1">

                <pb facs="0428" n="394" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v15p1/pg394"/>


                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Copy of Chou En-lai “Alternative B”</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-07-09">July 9, 1952</date>.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>“UNC would accept in principle that all prisoners of war on conclusion of
                    armistice could return to their homes; those who elected otherwise could be
                    brought to Panmunjom, not under military escort, and interviewed there by
                    Committee from ‘neutral states agreed upon’ plus Red Cross from both sides.
                    Chinese and North Koreans would abide by views of prisoners of war as expressed
                    in these interviews. Chou would discuss such a proposal if U.S. (assumably UNC)
                    agreed that prima facie all prisoners of war must be assumed desire repatriation
                    and only <pb facs="0429" n="395" xml:id="pg395"/> those who oppose, after being
                    freed of military influence and control of KMT and Rhee agents, should be
                    interviewed by independent body.”</p>
                <p>Another report of this conversation also includes in alternative B that there
                    must be agreement in principle on the provisions of the Geneva Convention, with
                    the proposed procedure described as being in implementation of these provisions.
                    This other report also contains no mention of Red Cross, speaks of “neutral
                    place” instead of mentioning Panmunjom specifically as the place for
                    interviewing prisoners of war not desiring repatriation, and speaks in terms of
                    recommendations by neutral Committee instead of determination by Committee of
                    desires of prisoners of war.</p>
            </div>
